1. 🌱 Boosting Theory
Bagging vs Boosting
	Bagging (Random Forest)	Boosting
How trees are built	In parallel	One after another
Main goal	Reduce variance	Reduce bias
Trees used	Deep	Shallow (depth 3-8)
Overfitting risk	Low	Higher
<br>
AdaBoost (the intuition)
Give every sample an equal weight.
Train a weak learner.
Increase the weight of misclassified samples.
The next learner focuses on those hard cases.
<br>
Gradient Boosting

Each new tree is added to the previous model, scaled by the learning rate η:

F_m(x) = F_(m-1)(x) + η · h_m(x)

The new tree h_m is trained on the residuals, the negative gradient of the loss:

r = - ∂L / ∂F
Regression (squared error): residual = y - prediction
Classification (log loss): residual = y - predicted probability

[!TIP] Think of it as "fix the mistakes of the previous trees, a little at a time."

<br>
The algorithm
Start with a constant (mean, or log-odds for classification).
Compute residuals.
Fit a small tree to them.
Add it to the model, scaled by η.
Repeat.
<br>
Key knobs
learning_rate: smaller is safer but needs more trees
n_estimators: more trees lower bias but can overfit
max_depth: controls model complexity
subsample: row sampling per tree, which reduces variance

[!IMPORTANT] learning_rate and n_estimators are coupled. Use a low rate (0.01-0.1) and let early stopping pick the number of trees.

<br>
2. ⚔️ XGBoost vs LightGBM vs CatBoost
XGBoost
Regularized objective with second-order gradients
Learns a default direction for missing values
Stable, reliable, the benchmark standard
LightGBM
Histogram binning, so it is very fast
Leaf-wise tree growth, which can overfit small data
Main parameter to watch: num_leaves
CatBoost
Ordered boosting to reduce target leakage
Best native categorical handling
Strong defaults, so little tuning is needed
<br>
Which one to pick?
Situation	Choose
Safe general benchmark	XGBoost
Huge dataset, need speed	LightGBM
Many categorical columns	CatBoost
No time to tune	CatBoost
<br>
sklearn-compatible API
python
from xgboost import XGBClassifier
from lightgbm import LGBMClassifier
from catboost import CatBoostClassifier

xgb  = XGBClassifier(n_estimators=500, learning_rate=0.05, max_depth=4)
lgbm = LGBMClassifier(n_estimators=500, learning_rate=0.05, num_leaves=31, verbose=-1)
cat  = CatBoostClassifier(iterations=500, learning_rate=0.05, depth=6, verbose=0)

They all support fit, predict, and predict_proba, so they work with Pipeline, GridSearchCV, and SHAP.

