3. 🌍 Real-World Issues
Missing values

All three libraries handle NaN natively, so imputation is optional. Sometimes it even helps, because "missing" can be a useful signal.

<br>
Imbalanced data
Library	Parameter
XGBoost	scale_pos_weight = n_neg / n_pos
LightGBM	is_unbalance=True or scale_pos_weight
CatBoost	auto_class_weights="Balanced"
python
ratio = (y_train == 0).sum() / (y_train == 1).sum()
model = XGBClassifier(scale_pos_weight=ratio)

[!NOTE] SMOTE and class weights both distort predicted probabilities. If you need real probabilities, calibrate (see Section 6).

<br>
Categorical features
Label/ordinal encoding works fine for trees.
Avoid one-hot on high-cardinality columns.
Native support:
LightGBM: category dtype
CatBoost: cat_features=[...]
XGBoost: enable_categorical=True
<br>
Monotonic constraints

Force the prediction to only go up (or only down) as a feature increases. Very useful in credit and risk models. For example, higher income should never increase default risk.

python
# 1 = increasing, -1 = decreasing, 0 = no constraint
XGBClassifier(monotone_constraints=(1, -1, 0))
