5. 🎯 Hyperparameter Tuning
Why GridSearch does not scale

5 parameters x 5 values = 3,125 combinations, each multiplied by the CV folds. Most of that effort is wasted.

<br>
RandomizedSearchCV

Samples random combinations, which is usually just as good with far fewer runs.

python
from sklearn.model_selection import RandomizedSearchCV
from scipy.stats import randint, uniform, loguniform

param_dist = {
    "model__n_estimators":     randint(200, 800),
    "model__learning_rate":    loguniform(0.01, 0.3),
    "model__max_depth":        randint(3, 9),
    "model__subsample":        uniform(0.6, 0.4),
    "model__colsample_bytree": uniform(0.5, 0.5),
}

search = RandomizedSearchCV(pipe, param_dist, n_iter=50,
                            scoring="average_precision", cv=5, n_jobs=-1)
search.fit(X_train, y_train)
<br>
Optuna (Bayesian optimization)
Learns which regions of the search space score well, then samples there
Pruning stops bad trials early
You define the search space with trial.suggest_*
<details> <summary><b>Click to expand: full Optuna example</b></summary> <br>
python
import optuna
from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score, StratifiedKFold

def objective(trial):
    params = {
        "n_estimators":     trial.suggest_int("n_estimators", 200, 1000),
        "learning_rate":    trial.suggest_float("learning_rate", 0.01, 0.3, log=True),
        "max_depth":        trial.suggest_int("max_depth", 3, 9),
        "min_child_weight": trial.suggest_int("min_child_weight", 1, 10),
        "subsample":        trial.suggest_float("subsample", 0.6, 1.0),
        "colsample_bytree": trial.suggest_float("colsample_bytree", 0.5, 1.0),
        "reg_lambda":       trial.suggest_float("reg_lambda", 1e-2, 10, log=True),
    }

    pipe = Pipeline([
        ("prep",  preprocess),
        ("smote", SMOTE(random_state=42)),
        ("model", XGBClassifier(**params, random_state=42)),
    ])

    cv = StratifiedKFold(5, shuffle=True, random_state=42)
    return cross_val_score(pipe, X_train, y_train, cv=cv,
                           scoring="average_precision").mean()

study = optuna.create_study(direction="maximize")
study.optimize(objective, n_trials=60)

print(study.best_params)
print(study.best_value)
</details> <br>
Sensible tuning order
Fix learning_rate (about 0.1) and find the number of trees with early stopping
Tune tree structure: max_depth, min_child_weight
Tune sampling: subsample, colsample_bytree
Tune regularization: reg_lambda, reg_alpha, gamma
Lower the learning rate and increase the trees for the final model
<br>
Nested CV (honest performance estimate)

Tuning and reporting on the same folds gives an optimistic score.

Inner loop: hyperparameter search
Outer loop: scores the whole tuning procedure
python
inner = StratifiedKFold(3, shuffle=True, random_state=1)
outer = StratifiedKFold(5, shuffle=True, random_state=2)

search = RandomizedSearchCV(pipe, param_dist, n_iter=20, cv=inner,
                            scoring="average_precision")
scores = cross_val_score(search, X_train, y_train, cv=outer,
                         scoring="average_precision")
print(scores.mean(), scores.std())

[!TIP] Nested CV is expensive. Use it for the final model comparison, not every experiment.

