6. 📊 Probability Calibration
The problem

A model can rank well (high ROC-AUC) but output probabilities that mean nothing. If it says 0.8, then about 80% of those cases should truly be positive.

Why it matters: risk scoring, expected-cost decisions, threshold tuning, and explaining results to stakeholders.

<br>
Who is usually miscalibrated?
✅ Logistic regression: usually well calibrated
⚠️ Random forest: under-confident near 0 and 1
⚠️ Boosted trees: often distorted, especially with SMOTE or class weights
❌ SVM and Naive Bayes: typically poor
<br>
Calibration curve (reliability diagram)

Bin the predictions, then plot predicted probability against the actual fraction of positives. The diagonal is perfect calibration.

python
from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

frac_pos, mean_pred = calibration_curve(y_val, p_val, n_bins=10, strategy="quantile")

plt.plot(mean_pred, frac_pos, marker="o", label="model")
plt.plot([0, 1], [0, 1], "--", label="perfect")
plt.legend(); plt.show()
<br>
Brier score

Mean squared error of the probabilities. Lower is better.

Brier = mean( (p - y)² )
python
from sklearn.metrics import brier_score_loss
brier_score_loss(y_val, p_val)
<br>
Two calibration methods

Platt scaling (method="sigmoid")

Fits a logistic regression on the model's scores
Best for small data

Isotonic regression (method="isotonic")

Flexible, non-parametric
Best for larger data (roughly 1000+ samples), can overfit on small data
<br>
CalibratedClassifierCV
python
from sklearn.calibration import CalibratedClassifierCV

cal = CalibratedClassifierCV(best_model, method="isotonic", cv=5)
cal.fit(X_train, y_train)

p_cal = cal.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]

[!IMPORTANT]

Calibrate on data the model has not been trained on
Calibration changes probabilities, not the ranking, so ROC-AUC barely moves but Brier improves
Calibrate before tuning the decision threshold
